   "post_data": {
    "post_id": 234080,
    "post_type": "forum",
    "topic_id": 37423,
    "poster_id": 1991,
    "post_rendered": "a.) For all positive integer <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c325612684d41304b9751c175df7bcc0f61f64f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k$\"  width=\"9\" height=\"12\" > find the smallest positive integer <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/1/2/81232ecd8d9e4b5c0f24a00dabec238068f26de9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f(k)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"33\" height=\"18\" > such that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/0/79069377f91364c2f87a64e5f9f562a091c8a6c1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5$\"  width=\"8\" height=\"12\" > sets <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/e/a/aead9be15e07cfac809e563c05a77678d4fc7ee6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$s_1,s_2, \\ldots , s_5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"95\" height=\"11\" > exist satisfying:<br>\n<br>\n<b>i.</b> each has <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c325612684d41304b9751c175df7bcc0f61f64f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k$\"  width=\"9\" height=\"12\" > elements;<br>\n<b>ii.</b> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/3/b/d3b63c17213f84d730e0de1f8c08597f7a648e37.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$s_i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" > and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/0/b/60b1a553231ce590a7199e467592ea789bc1dbeb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$s_{i+1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"28\" height=\"12\" > are disjoint for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/0/d/d0d419f5c784ce697beafbc0b00dbc520ae82e8a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$i=1,2,...,5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"96\" height=\"16\" > <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\">(<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/0/3/e03b22f1adc787a6a4ae7af4b99cf9a0487df9da.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$s_6=s_1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"54\" height=\"10\" >)</span><br>\n<b>iii.</b> the union of the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/0/79069377f91364c2f87a64e5f9f562a091c8a6c1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5$\"  width=\"8\" height=\"12\" > sets has exactly <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/1/2/81232ecd8d9e4b5c0f24a00dabec238068f26de9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f(k)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"33\" height=\"18\" > elements.<br>\n<br>\nb.) Generalisation: Consider <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/9/e/49e6a2d706867d6652d7edadccc3fd034130bd98.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n \\geq 3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"43\" height=\"15\" > sets instead of <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/0/79069377f91364c2f87a64e5f9f562a091c8a6c1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5$\"  width=\"8\" height=\"12\" >.</span>",
    "post_canonical": "a.) For all positive integer $k$ find the smallest positive integer $f(k)$ such that $5$ sets $s_1,s_2, \\ldots , s_5$ exist satisfying:\n \n[b]i.[/b] each has $k$ elements;\n[b]ii.[/b] $s_i$ and $s_{i+1}$ are disjoint for $i=1,2,...,5$ ($s_6=s_1$)\n[b]iii.[/b] the union of the $5$ sets has exactly $f(k)$ elements.\n\nb.) Generalisation: Consider $n \\geq 3$ sets instead of $5$.",
    "username": "orl",
    "post_number": 1,
    "post_format": "bbcode",
    "category_name": "High School Olympiads",
    "category_id": 6,
    "poster_avatar": "//avatar.artofproblemsolving.com/avatar_1991.jpg"
   }
  },
  {
   "item_id": 234248,
   "item_text": "Click for solution",
   "item_forum_id": 0,
   "item_type": "post_hidden",
   "in_feed": null,
   "is_watched": null,
   "item_score": "996",
   "id": "27409",
   "item_level": "996",
   "post_data": {
    "post_id": 234248,
    "post_type": "forum",
    "topic_id": 37423,
    "poster_id": 651,
    "post_rendered": "Just a simple observation: if <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\"  width=\"10\" height=\"8\" > is even, we can take <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/4/b/84b0c7fa82018ed8f38c9196cf69a8b4f0ce0de4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$s_1=s_3=...s_{n-1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"128\" height=\"10\" > and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/e/a/5ea5b911df352cc5dfd07a7f10f2c89b9513e500.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$s_2=s_4=...s_n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"112\" height=\"10\" > so we need at most <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/8/e/b8ea7ee8a282949fd853a01537ec7a2b55e56f75.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2k$\"  width=\"18\" height=\"12\" > different elements! It is obvius we cant do it with less than that number of elements!<br>\n<br>\nNow for odd <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\"  width=\"10\" height=\"8\" >,</span> each element appears at most in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/8/1/e81417cdf462da47d08c54d86bd14a4b25c723d1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{n-1}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"44\" height=\"37\" > sets, so we get <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/0/3/70335b40c3322b242d5d41a41b37a49bfe134142.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f(k)\\geq \\frac{2n}{n-1}k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"115\" height=\"37\" > I am not awake like to give a construction right now...",
    "post_canonical": "Just a simple observation: if $n$ is even, we can take $s_1=s_3=...s_{n-1}$ and $s_2=s_4=...s_n$ so we need at most $2k$ different elements! It is obvius we cant do it with less than that number of elements!\r\n\r\nNow for odd $n$, each element appears at most in $\\frac{n-1}{2}$ sets, so we get $f(k)\\geq \\frac{2n}{n-1}k$ I am not awake like to give a construction right now...",
    "username": "Pascual2005",
    "post_number": 2,
    "post_format": "bbcode",
    "category_name": "High School Olympiads",
    "category_id": 6,
    "poster_avatar": "//avatar.artofproblemsolving.com/avatar_651.jpg"
   }
