    "post_rendered": "Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/a/9/aa93788703edb553a06374a782532fac0ca6e4b9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ G$\"  width=\"14\" height=\"12\" > be a simple graph with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/3/9/f39ca614e6beed9720b862df639ee9a298f10980.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ 2 \\cdot n$\"  width=\"32\" height=\"12\" > vertices and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/f/5/bf5dfd1b7b935ae8914f9cec5374e32d45e1ef14.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ n^{2}+1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"48\" height=\"16\" > edges. Show that this graph <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/a/9/aa93788703edb553a06374a782532fac0ca6e4b9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ G$\"  width=\"14\" height=\"12\" > contains a <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/2/f/92f25a79fdd5c5eeccc27dc27644018955292e16.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ K_{4}-\\text{one edge}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"107\" height=\"15\" >,</span> that is, two triangles with a common edge.",
    "post_canonical": "Let $ G$ be a simple graph with $ 2 \\cdot n$ vertices and $ n^{2}+1$ edges. Show that this graph $ G$ contains a $ K_{4}-\\text{one edge}$, that is, two triangles with a common edge.",
